      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the smallest possible degree of a polynomial with rational coefficients that has the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as its roots. The reason we exclude 1001 is that it simplifies to just 1001 in a polynomial with rational coefficients.\n\nSince the polynomial has rational coefficients, if \\(1000 + \\sqrt{1001}\\) is a root, then its conjugate \\(1000 - \\sqrt{1001}\\) must also be a root. The same applies to \\(1 + \\sqrt{2}\\), \\(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\), and so on.\n\nLet's consider the pattern:\n- The polynomial will have roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}\\), \\(1-\\sqrt{2}\\), \\(2+\\sqrt{3}\\), \\(2-\\sqrt{3}\\), ..., \\(1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\), and \\(1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\).\n\nThis means the polynomial has a root for each \\(n + \\sqrt{n+1}\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 1000, and also a root for \\(1000 + \\sqrt{1001}\\), \\(1000 - \\sqrt{1001}\\).\n\nThus, the polynomial will have \\(2 \\times 1000 = 2000\\) roots, and one additional root \\(1000 + \\sqrt{1001}\\). Therefore, the smallest possible degree of the polynomial is \\(2000 + 1 = 2001\\).\n\nLet's confirm this reasoning by counting the roots explicitly. The polynomial will have:\n\n\\[2 \\times (1000 - 1) + 1 = 2 \\times 999 + 1 = 1999\\] roots that take the form \\(n + \\sqrt{n+1}\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 1000.\n\nSo, the polynomial must have a total of \\(1999 + 1 = 2000\\) roots.\n\nThus, the smallest possible degree of the polynomial is \\(\\boxed{2001}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code to ensure the reasoning is accurate.\n```python\n# The polynomial has roots of the form n + sqrt(n+1) for n from 1 to 1000, plus the root 1000 + sqrt(1001)\nnum_roots = 1000\n# Calculate the number of roots of the form n + sqrt(n+1)\nnum_roots_form = 2 * num_roots\n# The polynomial also includes the root 1000 + sqrt(1001)\nadditional_root = 1\n# Total number of roots\ntotal_roots = num_roots_form + additional_root\n# The smallest possible degree of the polynomial is the total number of roots\nprint(total_roots)\n```",
