      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the smallest possible degree of a nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients that has the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we need to recognize that for the polynomial to have rational coefficients, its roots must include both the irrational and their conjugates. Specifically, if \\(1+\\sqrt{2}\\) is a root, then \\(1-\\sqrt{2}\\) must also be a root. This pattern continues for each pair of numbers.\n\nGiven the sequence \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\), each number in the sequence has a corresponding pair \\((n, n+1) + \\sqrt{n+2}\\) that must also be roots. Therefore, for the polynomial to have rational coefficients, we need to include both \\(n+\\sqrt{n+2}\\) and \\(n-\\sqrt{n+2}\\) for each \\(n\\) from 1 to 1000.\n\nSince there are 1000 numbers in the sequence, there are also 1000 corresponding conjugate pairs. Therefore, the polynomial must have at least 2000 roots. The smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is thus \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).\n\nLet's verify this reasoning with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# The roots are of the form n + sqrt(n+2) and n - sqrt(n+2) for n from 1 to 1000\n# For each n, we need to have both n + sqrt(n+2) and n - sqrt(n+2) as roots\n# This means the polynomial must have at least 2000 roots in total\n\n# Python code to calculate the number of roots\nnumber_of_roots = 1000  # Each n from 1 to 1000 contributes 2 roots\nsmallest_possible_degree = number_of_roots * 2\nprint(smallest_possible_degree)\n```",
